### Code Implementation Guidelines
Follow these rules when you write code:
- Use early returns whenever possible to make the code more readable.
- Always use Tailwind classes for styling HTML elements; avoid using CSS or tags.
- Use “class:” instead of the tertiary operator in class tags whenever possible.
- Use descriptive variable and function/const names. Also, event functions should be named with a “handle” prefix, like “handleClick” for onClick and “handleKeyDown” for onKeyDown.
- Implement accessibility features on elements. For example, a tag should have a tabindex=“0”, aria-label, on:click, and on:keydown, and similar attributes.
- Use existing components code as a guide and follow similar coding style and rules.
- Storybook component should be seperated into three files and following rules for each files should be used.
     1. Componentname.stories.tsx - This file contains storybook component.
     2. Componentname.mock-data.ts - This file contains mock data
     3. Componentname.test.tsx - This file contains jest snapshot tests based on the same mock data, as well as functional unit tests.
- When styling, use TailwindVariants and use themed tailwind classes as defined in [tailwind.config.js](mdc:headapps/nextjs-starter/tailwind.config.js) and [theme-config.ts](mdc:headapps/nextjs-starter/theme-config.ts).
- When creating components use the wrappers defined in [NextJS app root path]/src/helpers/SitecoreWrappers when rendering fields or placeholders.  Refer to existing examples.
- For data models, reference the appropriate types under [NextJS app root path]/src/.generated.



### Unit Testing
- When creating unit tests for a React Component, it should live next to the stories and use the same mock data as the stories.
- Component testing should always include a snapshot test.  In addition to the snapshot, add checks for other expected functionality.
- Do not mock any SitecoreWrappers helper components.
- Do not use ever use getByTestId or getAllByTestId.  Instead when looking for components, use a DOM query by the "data-component" attribute
